        <div class="cav">The payoff</div>
        <h2 id="tax">What is credit card data worth at tax time?</h2>
        <p>For anyone with business expenses, a clean spreadsheet of card charges is exactly what the IRS asks for. IRS Publication 463 states that "you must have documentary evidence" for expenses, and that acceptable proof is <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p463" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">"a canceled check, credit card receipt, or written statement showing the name of the vendor, the date, and the amount."</a> That vendor, date, and amount triple is precisely what each exported row gives you, ready to sort into Schedule C categories. One caution worth stating plainly: a statement proves that you paid, but the IRS notes that "proof of payment of an amount, by itself, does not establish you are entitled to a tax deduction," so you still pair the line with a receipt or invoice showing the business purpose (<a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-pdf/p583.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS Publication 583</a>). The IRS also recommends keeping records generally for <a href="https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/how-long-should-i-keep-records" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">three years</a>, and separating business and personal accounts because it "makes it easier to keep records." A spreadsheet you can filter and total does both jobs at once.</p>
