        <div class="cav">The quiet leak</div>
        <h2 id="subscriptions">Can it help you find recurring charges you forgot?</h2>
        <p>Yes, and this is where a sortable export earns its keep fastest. People badly underestimate what they spend on subscriptions: a 2022 C+R Research survey of 1,000 US consumers found that people estimated about 86 dollars a month but actually spent about 219 dollars, a gap of 133 dollars a month they did not realize was leaving their account (<a href="https://www.crresearch.com/blog/subscription-service-statistics-and-costs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">C+R Research</a>). Those charges hide in plain sight on a statement because they are small and regular. Export a few months, sort by description, and the repeats line up: the streaming service you stopped watching, the app trial that converted, the tool you meant to cancel. It matters because cancelling is often made deliberately hard. The CFPB has warned about sellers who "erect unreasonable barriers to cancellation" and place callers "on hold for an unreasonably long time" (<a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/compliance/circulars/consumer-financial-protection-circular-2023-01-unlawful-negative-option-marketing-practices/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CFPB Circular 2023-01</a>). Seeing the charge in a spreadsheet is the push most people need to actually stop it.</p>
        <div class="callout"><div class="h">While you are reviewing, watch for errors too</div><p>The same sorted export makes unauthorized or duplicate charges easy to spot. If you find one, you have a deadline: both the CFPB and the FTC say you must send a written billing-error notice within 60 days after the statement with the error was sent (<a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/consumer-tools/credit-cards/how-to-fix-mistakes-in-your-credit-card-bill/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CFPB</a>). A spreadsheet history makes that far easier to catch in time.</p></div>

        <div class="cav">The locked drawer</div>
        <h2 id="history">How do you get the older statements your issuer keeps in PDF?</h2>
        <p>You download them as PDF and convert them, because most issuers only show recent activity in a tidy on-screen table and archive the rest as statement PDFs. How far back the on-screen view goes varies a lot by issuer, which is why a converter that reads the PDF is what unlocks the older history. American Express shows only your <a href="https://www.americanexpress.com/us/customer-service/faq.transaction-billing-statements-history.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">"previous two years of transactions"</a> electronically, and Citi gives you <a href="https://www.citi.com/online-services/bank-statements" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">"up to 24 months of online statements."</a> Bank of America lets you <a href="https://www.bankofamerica.com/deposits/account-statements-faqs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">"view, print and download up to 18 months"</a> of statements. Chase and Capital One are more generous, each keeping up to <a href="https://www.chase.com/personal/mobile-online-banking/login-paperless" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">seven years</a> of statements online. Once you have the PDFs in hand, the tool turns any of them into rows you can total, no matter which issuer they came from.</p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Issuer</th><th>Recent activity you can view online</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="band">Chase</td><td>Up to 7 years of statements</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">Capital One</td><td>Up to 7 years for active accounts</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">American Express</td><td>2 years of transactions, older ones archived as PDF</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">Citi</td><td>Up to 24 months of statements</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">Bank of America</td><td>18 months to view and download</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <div class="cav">The honest comparison</div>
        <h2 id="different">How is it different from an online converter?</h2>
        <p>The core difference is that the Best Answer Hub tool parses your statement in your browser and reconciles the result, while a typical online converter uploads the file to its servers and hands back rows you have to trust. Uploading adds a retention window and a third party to a document that holds your card number; skipping the totals check leaves you to eyeball whether every charge made it across. This tool has no upload, no watermark, no task cap, batch support for several statements, and the built-in reconciliation that tells you the export is complete. The table sets the usual experience next to this one.</p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>What you get</th><th>Best Answer Hub</th><th>Typical online converter</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Where the file is processed</td><td class="band">In your browser</td><td>Uploaded to a server</td></tr>
            <tr><td>File retained on a server</td><td class="band">None</td><td>1 to 2 hours (Smallpdf, iLovePDF)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Totals reconciled to the statement</td><td class="band">Yes, built in</td><td>Rarely, you check by hand</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Excel and CSV export</td><td class="band">Both</td><td>Often one, or paid</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Several statements at once</td><td class="band">Batch</td><td>Usually one at a time</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Works offline</td><td class="band">Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <div class="callout"><div class="h">Pair it with the rest of the toolkit</div><p>The Best Answer Hub <a href="/finance-toolkit/">Finance Toolkit</a> has the neighbors you reach for next: a <a href="/tools/bank-statement-to-excel/">Bank Statement to Excel</a> converter for your checking and savings history, and the wider <a href="/tools/">Tools hub</a> for the rest. Each runs in the browser and sends nothing.</p></div>

        <div class="cta">
          <span class="kicker">Convert it now</span>
          <h3>Open the Credit Card Statement to Excel tool</h3>
          <p>Free, no signup, and parsed entirely in your browser. Drop in a statement PDF, watch the rows reconcile to the printed total, and export to Excel or CSV without uploading a thing.</p>
          <a class="btn" href="/tools/credit-card-statement-to-excel/">Convert your statement</a>
        </div>

